# Target C — Section 151 candidate: internal-face isoperimetry excludes the Section-148 lower-edge endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Base:** hostile-audited proof-only Sections 148 and 150; canonical Sections 75, 82, 117/118 Type-II carrier area  
**Status:** proof-only endpoint-exclusion theorem; C-II remains open

The Section-150 endpoint cannot actually occur.

The missing observation is elementary but strong: Eppstein's component slack has an exact planar face interpretation. If `L_r^I` is the occurrence-counted outer-face length of the internal contact graph `C[I_r]` and `Q_r^I` is its bounded-face degree excess, then

\[
\boxed{
r_r={L_r^I+Q_r^I\over2}-\delta_r.}
\tag{151.1}
\]

Hence

\[
L_r^I\le2(\delta_r+r_r).
\tag{151.2}
\]

The total Euclidean area of all bounded faces of `C[I_r]` is at most

\[
{(L_r^I)^2\over4\pi}.
\]

In particular, if `A_{4,r}^{int}` denotes the total area of strict-rhombus faces whose four vertices lie in `I_r`, then

\[
\boxed{
A_{4,r}^{int}
\le{(\delta_r+r_r)^2\over\pi}.
}
\tag{151.3}
\]

Equivalently, with

\[
\eta_r:=\delta_r+2-d_r\ge0,
\]

\[
\boxed{
r_r+\eta_r
\ge
\sqrt{\pi A_{4,r}^{int}}+2-d_r.}
\tag{151.4}
\]

Now apply this to the Section-118 endpoint wrapping carrier. The sharp hostile-reviewed Section-118 area budget gives for the same carrier

\[
{A_{4,*}\over D^2}
\ge0.4703752868004241\ldots-o(1).
\tag{151.5}
\]

Only `O(D)` strict rhombi of that harmonic cell can touch loaded attachment vertices, because the attachment set has `O(D)` vertices and every loaded vertex has contact degree at most three. Every unit rhombus has area at most one. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
{A_{4,*}^{int}\over D^2}
\ge0.4703752868004241\ldots-o(1).}
\tag{151.6}
\]

At the safe Section-118 endpoint,

\[
{d_*\over D}	o
\lambda_*=0.9762439137425688\ldots .
\tag{151.7}
\]

Thus (151.4) forces

\[
\boxed{
{r_*+\eta_*\over D}
\ge
\sqrt{\pi\cdot0.4703752868004241\ldots}
-0.9762439137425688\ldots-o(1)
}
\tag{151.8}
\]

with numerical margin

\[
\boxed{
{r_*+\eta_*\over D}
\ge0.239374260456223\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{151.9}
\]

But hostile-audited Section 150 says every Section-148 lower-edge countersequence has

\[
R_I=o(D),
\qquad
\sum_r\eta_r=o(D).
\]

Since `0<=r_*<=R_I` and `0<=eta_*<=sum eta_r`, this gives

\[
r_*+\eta_*=o(D),
\]

contradicting (151.9).

Therefore:

### Theorem 151.1 — the strengthened Type-II lower edge is unattainable

There is no Type-II endpoint countersequence simultaneously satisfying

\[
{d_*\over D}\to0.9762439137425688\ldots,
\qquad
{1\over D}\sum_rd_r\to2.798596312209462\ldots,
\]

and

\[
{h\over D}\to1.721247148049524\ldots
\]

with the Section-118 endpoint carrier and Section-148 gap-moment structure.

Equivalently, the Section-148 scalar lower edge has a genuine positive stability gap once internal-face geometry is retained.

This does **not** yet close Type II: larger wrapping-carrier parameter regimes can lower the complement-gap diameter moment and require a new all-regime scalar optimization. But the formerly surviving lower-edge model is impossible by a direct finite planar argument.

---

## 151.1 Exact face formula for the Eppstein slack

Fix one unloaded component `I_r`. Let

- `n_r=|I_r|`;
- `e_r` be its internal unit contacts;
- `delta_r` its contact-graph diameter;
- `L_r^I` the occurrence-counted outer-face walk length of the connected plane graph `C[I_r]`; and
- `Q_r^I=sum_f(deg f-4)` over bounded nonquadrilateral faces of `C[I_r]`.

Triangle-freeness implies every bounded face has degree at least four. Euler and face incidence give exactly

\[
e_r
=2n_r-2-{L_r^I+Q_r^I\over2}.
\tag{151.10}
\]

Canonical Section 62 defines

\[
r_r:=2n_r-\delta_r-2-e_r\ge0.
\tag{151.11}
\]

Insert (151.10):

\[
r_r
={L_r^I+Q_r^I\over2}-\delta_r,
\]

proving (151.1). Since `Q_r^I>=0`,

\[
L_r^I\le2(\delta_r+r_r).
\]

No stability theorem for Eppstein's induction is used; this is simply Euler applied to the actual embedded internal graph.

---

## 151.2 Isoperimetry for a connected plane graph with bridges

Let `G` be any finite connected straight-line plane graph whose edges all have Euclidean length one, and let `L` be its occurrence-counted outer-face walk length.

Take the union of the closures of all bounded faces. Its positive-area connected components meet only at graph articulation points or along zero-area sets. Let their outer boundary lengths be `p_1,...,p_s`. Every physical edge on one such outer boundary is incident to the unbounded face on its other side and contributes at least one occurrence to the outer-face walk. Bridges contribute occurrences to `L` but no positive-area boundary credit. Therefore

\[
\sum_jp_j\le L.
\tag{151.12}
\]

Planar isoperimetry on each positive-area component gives

\[
A_j\le{p_j^2\over4\pi}.
\]

Hence

\[
\sum_jA_j
\le{1\over4\pi}\sum_jp_j^2
\le{1\over4\pi}\left(\sum_jp_j\right)^2
\le{L^2\over4\pi}.
\tag{151.13}
\]

Every bounded strict-rhombus face of `C[I_r]` lies in this bounded-face union. Combining (151.13) with (151.2) proves (151.3).

---

## 151.3 Loaded-touching rhombi cost only boundary order

Let `A_r` be the distinct loaded attachment set of the selected harmonic component and `a_r=|A_r|`. Canonical convex-perimeter/unit-separation bounds give

\[
a_r=O(D).
\tag{151.14}
\]

Every loaded vertex has full contact degree at most three. In a plane embedding, the number of bounded-face incidences at a loaded vertex is at most its contact degree, hence at most three. Therefore the number of strict-rhombus faces of the harmonic-cell graph incident to at least one loaded attachment is at most

\[
3a_r=O(D).
\tag{151.15}
\]

Every unit rhombus has area at most one. Deleting all loaded-touching rhombi therefore removes only `O(D)` total rhombus area.

Any remaining strict-rhombus face has all four vertices in `I_r`. Its four contact edges are internal edges, and deleting loaded vertices elsewhere cannot destroy that bounded face. Hence it is a bounded strict-rhombus face of `C[I_r]`.

Thus

\[
\boxed{
A_{4,r}^{int}
\ge A_{4,r}-O(D).
}
\tag{151.16}
\]

For the Section-118 endpoint carrier, (151.5) gives (151.6).

---

## 151.4 Numerical separation is large

At the sharp safe endpoint,

\[
A_*=0.4703752868004241\ldots,
\qquad
\lambda_*=0.9762439137425688\ldots .
\]

The isoperimetric graph-geodesicity threshold is

\[
\sqrt{\pi A_*}
=1.215618174198792\ldots .
\]

Therefore the mandatory internal slack is

\[
\sqrt{\pi A_*}-\lambda_*
=0.239374260456223\ldots .
\]

The margin is first-order and large. No delicate `10^-5` optimization is involved.

---

## 151.5 Scope and next frontier

- All source points are retained.
- The proof concerns the internal graph of one actual unloaded harmonic component; no contact-isolated point is inserted into it.
- Bridges and articulation points are allowed and are handled by occurrence-counted outer length plus componentwise isoperimetry.
- No generic squaregraph size-versus-diameter theorem is assumed.
- The Section-118 area value is the strict-rhombus area floor for the same carrier used in the endpoint theorem.
- The contradiction excludes the Section-148 **lower-edge endpoint**. It does not yet show an explicit universal improved `h/D` constant because away from the endpoint both the wrapping diameter moment and carrier area floor must be tracked simultaneously.

The immediate all-regime target is now a two-variable scalar theorem. For a wrapping carrier with parameter `theta`, keep simultaneously

1. its complement-gap diameter moment `S(theta)`;
2. its strict-rhombus area floor `A(theta)`; and
3. the component slack lower bound
   \[
   r_*+\eta_*
   \ge\sqrt{\pi A(theta)}-\sin(theta/2).
   \]

Insert this retained positive slack into the exact Section-148 elimination rather than dropping it. If the resulting scalar lower bound on `h/D` exceeds the global feasible upper envelope for every surviving `theta`, Type II closes.

**Theorem state:** unchanged; proof-only candidate pending hostile audit.
